 The user wants to clean all empty files and folders on C, E, and I drives. I'll write a PowerShell script for this.## How to Run This

1. Press **Windows key** → type **PowerShell**
2. Right-click → **Run as Administrator**
3. Paste the script → press **Enter**

---

## Step 1 — Preview First (Safe — Deletes Nothing)

Run this first to **see** what will be deleted before actually removing anything:

```powershell
# PREVIEW ONLY - shows empty files and folders, deletes nothing
$drives = @("C:\", "E:\", "I:\")

foreach ($drive in $drives) {
    if (Test-Path $drive) {
        Write-Host "`n=== Scanning $drive ===" -ForegroundColor Cyan

        # Empty files (0 bytes)
        $emptyFiles = Get-ChildItem -Path $drive -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { $_.Length -eq 0 }

        # Empty folders
        $emptyFolders = Get-ChildItem -Path $drive -Recurse -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { (Get-ChildItem $_.FullName -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Count -eq 0 }

        Write-Host "Empty files found:   $($emptyFiles.Count)" -ForegroundColor Yellow
        Write-Host "Empty folders found: $($emptyFolders.Count)" -ForegroundColor Yellow

        $emptyFiles   | Select-Object FullName | Format-Table -AutoSize
        $emptyFolders | Select-Object FullName | Format-Table -AutoSize
    } else {
        Write-Host "$drive not found - skipping" -ForegroundColor Red
    }
}
```

---

## Step 2 — Delete Them (Run After Reviewing Preview)

Once you're happy with what the preview showed, run this to actually delete:

```powershell
# DELETES empty files and folders on C, E, I drives
$drives = @("C:\", "E:\", "I:\")

# Safety: skip critical Windows system folders
$skipFolders = @("C:\Windows", "C:\Program Files", "C:\Program Files (x86)")

foreach ($drive in $drives) {
    if (Test-Path $drive) {
        Write-Host "`n=== Cleaning $drive ===" -ForegroundColor Cyan

        # Delete empty files
        $emptyFiles = Get-ChildItem -Path $drive -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object {
                $_.Length -eq 0 -and
                -not ($skipFolders | Where-Object { $_.FullName -like "$_*" })
            }

        foreach ($file in $emptyFiles) {
            try {
                Remove-Item $file.FullName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                Write-Host "Deleted file: $($file.FullName)" -ForegroundColor Gray
            } catch {
                Write-Host "Skipped (in use): $($file.FullName)" -ForegroundColor DarkYellow
            }
        }

        # Delete empty folders (sorted deepest first so nested empties get caught)
        $emptyFolders = Get-ChildItem -Path $drive -Recurse -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { (Get-ChildItem $_.FullName -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Count -eq 0 } |
            Sort-Object { $_.FullName.Length } -Descending

        foreach ($folder in $emptyFolders) {
            try {
                Remove-Item $folder.FullName -Force -Recurse -ErrorAction Stop
                Write-Host "Deleted folder: $($folder.FullName)" -ForegroundColor Gray
            } catch {
                Write-Host "Skipped (protected): $($folder.FullName)" -ForegroundColor DarkYellow
            }
        }

        Write-Host "=== $drive done ===" -ForegroundColor Green
    } else {
        Write-Host "$drive not found - skipping" -ForegroundColor Red
    }
}

Write-Host "`nAll drives cleaned!" -ForegroundColor Green
```

---

## Important Notes

| | |
|---|---|
| ✅ **Run Step 1 first** | Always preview before deleting |
| ✅ **C:\Windows is protected** | Script skips critical system folders |
| ✅ **"Skipped" messages are normal** | Means Windows protected those files |
| ⚠️ **I: drive** | Script will say "not found" if that drive isn't plugged in |

Run **Step 1 first**, check the list looks sensible, then run **Step 2**. Let me know what it finds!

